    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d349">

                <pb facs="0412" n="406" sameAs="frus:frus1942v03/pg406"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/18301: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Thurston</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kuibyshev</hi> (<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi>)</placeName>,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d349-f1"/>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1942-01-08T11:00:00+03:00">January 8,
                            1942—11 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 9—4:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>25. I have received from Molotov<anchor n="3" xml:id="d349-f2"/> a very long
                        note,<anchor n="4" xml:id="d349-f3"/> also a circular printed in the local
                    papers, concerning German looting, torture, murder, and destruction as revealed
                    by the Soviet reoccupation of areas overrun by the German forces. I shall send a
                    translation of this document by mail.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d349-f4"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Thurston</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d349-f1" n="2" xml:id="d349-f1-content">
                <p>The necessity for the removal of the American Embassy from Moscow to Kuibyshev in
                    October 1941, with a reduced staff remaining in Moscow, is explained in <ref
                        target="frus1941v01#pg_907">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1941, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, pp. 907</ref>–<ref
                        target="frus1941v01#pg_911">911</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d349-f2" n="3" xml:id="d349-f2-content">
                <p>Vyacheslav Mikhailovich Molotov, People’s Commissar for Foreign Affairs of the
                    Soviet Union.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d349-f3" n="4" xml:id="d349-f3-content">
                <p>This note of January 6, 1942, was translated in full in the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Moscow News</hi> of January 12, 1942 under the heading:
                    “Regarding the Wholesale Looting, Ruination of the Population and Monstrous
                    Atrocities Perpetrated by the German Authorities on Soviet Territories Seized by
                    Them.” For correspondence regarding Allied declarations concerning German
                    atrocities in occupied territories, see <ref target="frus1941v01#pg_445">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1941, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, pp. 445</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d349-f4" n="5" xml:id="d349-f4-content">
                <p>Despatch No. 1464 of January 12, 1942, containing lengthy excerpts of this note
                    in translation, not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
